# Copy to .env and fill in.
#
# These two differ in reach. EXPO_PUBLIC_DEV_TOKEN is behind __DEV__, so it
# folds to null in a release bundle. EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL is NOT — it is read in
# every build, and it is what a shipped app talks to.

# Which RunGuard backend this build talks to. Read ONCE, at bundle time —
# src/lib/api.ts does `process.env.EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL || "http://localhost:3000"`
# as a module-level const, so there is no runtime switch and no in-app field to
# change it. The value you build with is the value that ships.
#
#   local dev, iOS Simulator   http://localhost:3000   (shares the Mac's loopback)
#   local dev, physical phone  http://<your Mac's LAN IP>:3000
#   testing against Preview    the Vercel preview URL   → the DEV database
#   a release build            the production URL       → the PROD database
#
# A RELEASE BUILD MUST NOT RELY ON THE FALLBACK. Unset, it ships
# http://localhost:3000, which on a phone is the phone — every request fails.
# There is no health endpoint, so it does not error: it hangs for 15 seconds and
# then reports that the server is unreachable, on every screen, forever.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=

# Skips the login screen in dev: src/lib/auth.ts sends this as the Bearer when
# nothing is in the keychain. It is a SESSION TOKEN, obtained by signing in —
# the same value a real sign-in stores, and a full credential for whoever it
# names. Sign in as your own seeded athlete or team (`npm run seed` in website/)
# to get one, and never reuse someone else's.
#
# EITHER ROLE. `auth.ts` reads which from the token's own shape: `a.<id>.<hmac>`
# is an athlete, `<teamId>.<hmac>` is a coach. So switching the simulator
# between the athlete tabs and the coach tabs is editing this one line. One
# variable rather than two on purpose — two would be an athlete and a coach
# credential present at once, which `getCredential()` refuses outright.
EXPO_PUBLIC_DEV_TOKEN=
